# WorkerTransfer — the configuration, complete.
#
#   make env && make up
#
# `make env` writes a .env from this template and ROLLS the secrets fresh.
# Without .env `make up` aborts and says which key is missing — that is on
# purpose.
#
# THE SECRETS STAND EMPTY HERE, and that is the point: a built-in default is a
# secret that lies in git. Whoever copies this file by hand fills it in
# themselves — the command stands beside each one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHO READS THIS
#
#   docker compose  reads .env from the root by itself and fills in the
#                   ${...} places in docker-compose.yml.
#   a service       reads it via `EnvironmentFile.Load()` (DotNetEnv), the first
#                   line of every Program.cs — for the case that somebody starts
#                   a service on its own. In the container the environment
#                   comes from compose or from the Helm chart, and that wins: a
#                   variable that is already set is not overwritten.
#
# So the order of precedence is: set environment > .env > appsettings.json.
#
# Later Infisical fills the environment. It feeds this file, it does not
# replace the mechanism — nothing in the code changes for it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT DOES NOT STAND HERE
#
# Connection strings, service addresses and ports. docker-compose.yml knows
# those (in the cluster the chart), because they belong to the topology and not
# to one person's environment. Here stand secrets and the few values that
# differ per installation.


# ===========================================================================
# SECRETS — each with a reason of its own
# ===========================================================================

# What tokens are signed and verified with.
#
# The key pair tokens are signed and verified with (ECDSA P-256). `make env`
# rolls it; by hand:
#
#   openssl ecparam -name prime256v1 -genkey -noout -out k.pem
#   openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -nocrypt -in k.pem -outform DER | base64   # PRIVATE_KEY
#   openssl ec -in k.pem -pubout -outform DER | base64              # PUBLIC_KEY
#
# ONLY identity-service gets the private key. With a shared HS256 secret this
# difference would not exist: every service that can verify could also mint — a
# copied configuration sheet would then be every account on every service.
WORKERTRANSFER_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=

# All fifteen get the public one. It verifies and can do nothing else.
WORKERTRANSFER_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY=

# Names the key. A change runs by letting both ids hold side by side for a
# while — without a name it would be a cut at which every issued token becomes
# invalid at one stroke.
WORKERTRANSFER_JWT_KEY_ID=k1

# Who a service is at the doors of the others (ADR-0057).
#
# Each of the eleven calling services fetches short-lived tokens from
# identity-service with a secret of its own, and each door names who may enter.
# The doors are service-to-service entrances without a route in the gateway;
# whoever is not on a door's list gets 404 and not 401.
#
# A SECRET AND ITS HASH BELONG TOGETHER. The service gets the secret, identity
# only the hash, and two halves made apart do not fit. `make env` makes the
# pairs; `make env-m2m` adds missing ones to an existing .env and changes
# nothing else.
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_ADVISOR_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_ADVISOR_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_APPLICATIONS_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_APPLICATIONS_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_ASSESSMENT_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_ASSESSMENT_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_COMPANIES_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_COMPANIES_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_JOBS_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_JOBS_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_MESSAGES_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_MESSAGES_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_NOTIFICATION_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_NOTIFICATION_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_PROFILE_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_PROFILE_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_RESUME_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_RESUME_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_SCOUT_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_SCOUT_SECRET_HASH=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_TRANSFER_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_M2M_TRANSFER_SECRET_HASH=

# What the erasure cascade goes to its recipients with.
#
# EXPRESSLY A DIFFERENT ONE from the services' tokens: "may trigger a mail" and
# "may erase everything about a person" must not be the same paper (ADR-0027).
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
WORKERTRANSFER_ERASURE_SECRET=

# The master key stored secrets lie under — today exactly one: the AI key a
# person enters in their own settings. AES-GCM, a random vector per secret.
#
# ONLY identity-service gets it. Whoever holds it and the row in identity's
# database opens every person's AI key (ADR-0058).
#
# ROLLING IT MEANS LOSING THEM. There is no key versioning: a new master key
# makes every stored secret unreadable. The UI then shows "none stored", and
# the person enters a new one — that is the honest state for the first step,
# and it stands here so nobody triggers it by accident.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
WORKERTRANSFER_SECRETS_KEY=

# What the Postgres image demands on an empty volume. scripts/initdb ends it in
# the same run: the superuser has no network login (ADR-0058), and a database
# tool on this machine signs in with a service's role.
#
# `make env` rolls this and everything below; `make db-roles` adds what is
# missing to an existing .env and changes nothing else.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
WORKERTRANSFER_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# One login per service, into its own database and no other (ADR-0058).
#
# PLAIN HEX, unlike the secrets above: `=`, `+` and `/` would otherwise stand in a
# connection string.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_ADVISOR=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_APPLICATIONS=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_ASSESSMENT=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_COMPANIES=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_CONSENT=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_GITHUB=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_IDENTITY=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_JOBS=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_MESSAGES=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_NOTIFICATION=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_PORTFOLIO=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_PROFILE=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_RESUME=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_SCOUT=
WORKERTRANSFER_DB_PASSWORD_TRANSFER=

# The key each service's ASP.NET key ring lies under (ADR-0058). Not the master
# key: fourteen services needed that for this alone.
#
# Rolling one costs a sign-in with a foreign provider that is under way, and no
# stored data. Old entries in that service's `dataprotection_keys` then no longer
# open, and every start logs one error per entry until the table is emptied.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_ADVISOR=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_APPLICATIONS=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_ASSESSMENT=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_COMPANIES=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_CONSENT=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_GITHUB=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_IDENTITY=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_JOBS=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_MESSAGES=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_NOTIFICATION=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_PORTFOLIO=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_PROFILE=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_RESUME=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_SCOUT=
WORKERTRANSFER_KEYRING_KEY_TRANSFER=

# The secret each service's evidence lies behind — the operator report under
# `/noelia/` that `make evidence` and the Control Plane collect (ADR-0044).
#
# A PAPER OF ITS OWN, and that is a decision. It is expressly not the
# notification's and not the erasure's: "may trigger a mail", "may erase
# everything about a person" and "may see which providers this instance uses"
# are three different rights. And one per service (ADR-0058): a leaked one opens
# one report and not fifteen.
#
# EMPTY MEANS: the door is shut. Without this secret the report answers 404 —
# not 403, because an operations surface whose existence one can guess is
# already a disclosure in itself.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_ADVISOR=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_APPLICATIONS=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_ASSESSMENT=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_COMPANIES=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_CONSENT=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_GITHUB=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_IDENTITY=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_JOBS=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_MESSAGES=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_NOTIFICATION=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_PORTFOLIO=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_PROFILE=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_RESUME=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_SCOUT=
WORKERTRANSFER_EVIDENCE_SECRET_TRANSFER=


# ===========================================================================
# WHAT DIFFERS PER INSTALLATION
# ===========================================================================

# Which origins the browser may have (CORS), comma-separated.
#
# Without this key Noelia falls back to `http://localhost:3000` in Development —
# an origin that does not exist here. The UI lies on 5173, through the gateway
# on 8090.
WORKERTRANSFER_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:8090

# At which address people reach the UI.
#
# Stands in the mails — confirmation link, invitation. Set wrongly means: the
# mail arrives and the link leads nowhere.
WORKERTRANSFER_WEB_URL=http://localhost:5173

# A GitHub token for github-service.
#
# LEAVING IT EMPTY is valid and the normal case locally: without a token the
# service asks unauthenticated and runs into GitHub's tight rate limit. It says
# so instead of pretending it works.
WORKERTRANSFER_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Signing in via GitHub — it replaces the manual gist.
#
# LEAVING ALL THREE EMPTY is valid and the normal case locally: the UI then
# offers only the gist. Whoever wants to set it up creates an OAuth app under
# github.com/settings/developers and enters here what stands there. The
# redirect URL must be registered there WORD FOR WORD — GitHub refuses every
# other one.
#
# No permission is requested (`scope` stays empty): a single call is needed, to
# learn the login name. The access token is thrown away afterwards and stored
# nowhere.
WORKERTRANSFER_GITHUB_OAUTH_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_GITHUB_OAUTH_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_GITHUB_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:5173/github

# Signing in with Google, Microsoft, Apple (ADR-0053). EMPTY means off: no
# button appears. Each provider wants an app registered by the operator —
# Google Cloud Console (OAuth client, "Web application"), Microsoft Entra (app
# registration, accounts in any organisation and personal accounts), Apple
# Developer Program (Services ID + a "Sign in with Apple" key). The redirect
# address to register there is the gateway's:
#   ${WORKERTRANSFER_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/external/{google|microsoft|apple}/callback
# Only `openid email profile` is asked for (Apple: `name email`); no token is
# kept. Apple's key is the body of the .p8 file, base64, without its lines.
WORKERTRANSFER_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8090
WORKERTRANSFER_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
WORKERTRANSFER_APPLE_CLIENT_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_APPLE_TEAM_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_APPLE_KEY_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Files from Google Drive and Dropbox on the CV page (ADR-0054). EMPTY means no
# button. Only the chosen file is fetched — Google through the Picker with the
# `drive.file` scope (an OAuth client id for a web app, an API key restricted
# to the Picker API, and the project number as app id), Dropbox through the
# Chooser (an app key, no OAuth; register this site's domain with the app).
# These values are public by nature: they end up in every browser. OneDrive is
# deliberately missing — its picker reads ALL of a person's files.
WORKERTRANSFER_GOOGLE_PICKER_CLIENT_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_GOOGLE_PICKER_API_KEY=
WORKERTRANSFER_GOOGLE_PICKER_APP_ID=
WORKERTRANSFER_DROPBOX_APP_KEY=

# The platform's model — for the cover letter, the profile and advert help, the
# scout's approach and the reply drafts, for everybody who has not deposited a
# key of their own (ADR-0051). A person's own key (Anthropic or OpenAI, in the
# account settings) replaces it for everything she drafts.
#
# UNTIL THE START the platform's model is the operator's LOCAL OLLAMA. Nothing
# leaves the machine. Pull a model first (`ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:7b`, or any
# other) and name it below. Ollama reads no key — but the key is the switch:
# EMPTY means "the platform has no model", and the UI says so. For Ollama, set
# WORKERTRANSFER_AI_KEY=ollama.
#
# It stays empty here on purpose: CI and the E2E journeys measure a stack
# without a platform model (`make e2e` and `make route-map` switch it off for
# their run, scripts/without-model.sh).
#
# Which provider the platform runs in operation — and whether people get it by
# subscription or by quota — is decided before the start, with the business
# model (VISION-FRAGEN-2 Q1).
#
# Until 07.10.2026 the key was called ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. An old .env still
# carrying it is not read any more.
WORKERTRANSFER_AI_KEY=
WORKERTRANSFER_AI_PROVIDER=openai_compatible
WORKERTRANSFER_AI_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1/chat/completions
WORKERTRANSFER_AI_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:7b


# ===========================================================================
# LOCAL ONLY
# ===========================================================================

# How much room the auth brake has in the local stack.
#
# 1 is the default and the shipped value. Locally 40 stands here, because the
# Playwright journeys create 34 accounts — all from one origin, within a few
# minutes. A test bench is not the threat the brake is built against.
#
# DO NOT take this into the chart: no test bench runs there, and a factor in a
# staging environment would be a brake that only looks like one.
WORKERTRANSFER_BRAKE_FACTOR=40

# The licence of the Noelia Control Plane (overlay `docker-compose.noelia.yml`).
#
# Empty means: the free tier — one fleet, three services. This fleet has
# fourteen, so without a licence it stays incomplete.
#
# It stands here and not in `scripts/control-plane-fleet.json`: a signed paper
# naming a customer does not belong in git.
WORKERTRANSFER_NOELIA_LICENCE=
